<title>USA: High prices trigger special US cotton import quota.</title>
<headline>High prices trigger special US cotton import quota.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Continuing high cotton prices triggered a provision allowing the import of 44,522,887 kgs, or 98,156,244 lbs, of upland cotton, U.S. Agriculture Department said Friday.</p>
<p>The special cotton quota, effective from Dec 4, is equivalent to one week's consumption by U.S. mills. The quota was the latest to be announced by USDA during a run of high prices. None of the 23 special quotas now in effect has been filled, according to the U.S. Customs Service.</p>
<p>The latest quota applies to cotton purchased not later than March 3, 1997, and imported by June 1.</p>
